BIT-13: Hand pipes to threads dynamically in the reaction and dispersion loops

The reaction loop and the two dispersion loops gave each thread an equal
block of pipes, but a pipe's work is proportional to its number of
segments (1 to over 300), and two of an M1 Pro's cores are slower. Use a
dynamic schedule, a few pipes at a time. Each pipe's arithmetic is
unchanged, so results do not depend on which thread does it.

Requires: none
Results: identical (every concentration, the output file and the report are unchanged)

Apply from the root of an EPANET-MSX checkout at 85a303d, after FIX-01, FIX-02 and BIT-01 to BIT-12:
    git apply --whitespace=nowarn BIT-13.patch
or use tools/build-msx.sh, which applies the series in order.

diff --git a/src/solver/msxchem.c b/src/solver/msxchem.c
index ca1b6c1..a8e0a6e 100644
--- a/src/solver/msxchem.c
+++ b/src/solver/msxchem.c
@@ -514,14 +514,16 @@ int MSXchem_react(double dt)
     }
 
 // --- examine each link
-//     (the largest error code found is returned whatever the number
+//     (links are handed out to threads in small chunks since the
+//     number of segments, and so the work, varies a lot between links;
+//     the largest error code found is returned whatever the number
 //     of threads, as the order in which links are done may vary)
 
 #pragma omp parallel
 {
     int ierr;
     ChemState *cs = Chem;
-    #pragma omp for private(k)
+    #pragma omp for private(k) schedule(dynamic, 2)
     for (k = 1; k <= MSX.Nobjects[LINK]; k++)
     {
         // --- skip non-pipe links
